mishkin@atria.com (Nathaniel Mishkin) writes:

>Thuan Q. Pham (thuan@hpltp.hpl.hp.com) wrote:
>: If all servers in the DCE environment suppose to be stable, does this mean
>: we shouldn't, and not allowed to have instances of servers that can be
>: started to support a session of transaction, and then come down when it is
>: done?  

>..., I guess I wonder whether it's appropriate to export these
>transient servers into the namespace.  I think I can equally well
>(perhaps better) imagine a scheme where a "transaction starter" server
>is cataloged in the name space and you contact it and it tells you
>where the real server you want is.  Or something.  Not everything
>needs to be in the namespace.  Application-specific rendezvous schemes
>are bound to exist.

Conversely, I wonder why I should go to the trouble of writing an
application-specific name service when DCE provides CDS already.
Suppose I have a workgroup coordination application in which I
want each user to launch a server whenever they log in, and kill it
whenever they log out.  That creates a fair amount of coming and going
of servers.  If I implement my own rendezvous service I can bypass the
overhead of CDS information propagation, but every connection will
be delayed while the client makes 2 nameserver queries, one to CDS to
find the application-specific server, and then one to that server.
The proper approach in the long run would seem to be to
make CDS reasonably efficient.
