Ken Pierce (kop@uswest.com) wrote:
: 1.  Is there a means (or will there be a means in 1.1) for denying
:  access to a dce_client or principal after some number of unsuccessful
:  login attempts.

This is not a good idea.  It leaves you wide open to a denial-of-service
attack.  If I know your login name, I can lock you out by making N attempts
to log in as you.

A better idea is a "login attempt rate" limit.  This limits the number
of attempts than can be made on a password during its lifetime.  The
DoD recommends a rate between one per second and one per minute.  For
this and other sensible recommendations, get the "Department of Defense
Password Management Guideline, 12 April 1985."  The report number is
CSC-STD-002-85.

wunder


