This query seems a bit confused, so I'll try a little second-guessing,
but mostly suggest some clarification...

In article <28c9q7$b4j@menudo.uh.edu>, cscc1s@menudo.uh.edu (Arthur K.
S. Yeo) writes:
|> We need some references (to papers, etc.) or viewpoints
|> regarding a fair comparison of the OSF/DCE and ISODE with regards

First and foremost, DCE is a set of technology and ISODE is an implementation
of a set of technology.  Secondly, they don't strictly overlap.

DCE specifies remote procedure call, data representation, remote file
access, authentication and access control, and resource location 
(directory services).  ISODE as a reference usually means the implementation
of RFC1006, which specifies how to do TP0 over TCP.  The ISODE software
package is much more, containing a full set of OSI applications, as well
as session and presentation layer protocol code.  

So, it's a bit tough to figure out how to do a comparison.

To the extent that one looks for the common subset, then
presentation/representation and remote operations/procedure call are the
two points of commonality.

|> to the following items.
|> 
|> 1) How far ahead is it in becoming a standard?

ISODE implements things which are ISO standards, except for RFC 1006 which
is an Internet (IETF) standard.  DCE implements technologies chosen by
OSF.


|> 3) In what ways is one better than the other ?

Sorry to be pendantic, but you need to state some specific
criteria, or you will only get religious responses.  Probably will
get them, anyhow...




