Bruno.Richoux (brunor@cnmat5.frec.bull.fr) wrote:
: I would like to have the following information about using threads in the 
: following context : one multi-threaded UNIX process and several "normal"
: UNIX processes communicate data through a shared memory segment. This segment
: is accessed exclusively and is then protected by an IPC semaphore. The
: questions are :

: 1) Are the UNIX system calls semop and semctl blocking : if the semaphore is locked, a call to semop by a thread blocks the thread or blocks the process ?

: 2) Related to 1), are there jacket routines developped for primitives
: handling shared memory and / or semaphores and where can I get the
: information (I've got the DCE thread sources).

If you're talking about the straight DCE Threads implementation and
unless things changed a lot since last I looked at this stuff (9
months ago), the semaphore ops will block all the threads (i.e., the
whole process).  Short of polling, I don't think there's a way to
write jacket routines for the semaphore ops.  The problem is that UNIX
feature adders seemed to miss the point of having one "N-way wait"
primitive that can be used to wait on all things that could block
particular system calls.  So we have select() which works on file
descriptors but not semaphores, poll() which can be used to wait on
Streams-based things but not other things, and semaphores which are in
a class of their own.  For process death, you can more or less stumble
by with SIGCHLD, but it's less than satisfactory.  ptrace() events?
Sorry.

Presumably a real kernel-based threads implementation would get this
sort of thing right.  Windows NT did _and_ they made a single N-way
wait primitive (WaitForMultipleObjects()) as well.
