In article <301@vercors.frec.bull.fr> brunor@cnmat5.frec.bull.fr (Bruno.Richoux        ) writes:

> 1) Are the UNIX system calls semop and semctl blocking : if the semaphore is
> locked, a call to semop by a thread blocks the thread or blocks the process ?
> 
> 2) Related to 1), are there jacket routines developped for primitives
> handling shared memory and / or semaphores and where can I get the
> information (I've got the DCE thread sources).

If the platform you're running on uses the threads library from OSF, then
semop and semctl operations will block the whole process.  There is no
viable way to provide wrappers for them.

The only reason wrappers work for normal IO is because the select() system
call can be used to notify the process of IO being available.  There is no
equivalent mechanism for sem operations.

