In article <CDI44s.968@atria.com>, Nathaniel Mishkin <mishkin@atria.com> wrote:
++ poll() which can be used to wait on
++ Streams-based things but not other things,

	As far as I know, poll() in SVR4 can basically wait for the
same class of descriptors that select() can (in fact, I believe
select() is implemented atop poll(), though one must wonder what that
does for select()'s sec/usec timeval accuracy since poll() is
millisecond based...).

++ and semaphores which are in
++ a class of their own.

	The main incompatible culprits seem to be the System V IPC
mechanisms (i.e., message queues, semaphores, shared memory).  Trying
to write a program that combines message queues with e.g., sockets or
pipes is a nightmare, since message queues aren't "selectable" (ugh).  

++ Presumably a real kernel-based threads implementation would get this
++ sort of thing right.  Windows NT did _and_ they made a single N-way
++ wait primitive (WaitForMultipleObjects()) as well.

	NT also has the interesting (and potentially quite useful)
feature that all objects (e.g., synchronization objects, threads,
files, pipes, processes, etc.) are capable of being waited on.
Unfortunately, WaitForMultipleObjects isn't quite as useful as
select() or poll() since it only reports 1 object of the set of n
objects when it returns (select() and poll() also return the set of
objects that are now "enabled").  Moreover, it remains unclear whether
sockets fit into the WaitForMultipleObjects paradigm.  If they
*don't*, then we're basically back to the UNIX morass again...
	
