:   rpc_binding_vector_free()  /* if you still have binding vectors around */
: }

There is a school of thought that says that unexporting a server from
the CDS namespace is not necessarily such a good idea because in the
case of replicated directories the unexport operation (actually all
modifying operations) is moderately expensive (induces propagation
load).  And (so goes the school), why bother anyway?  In case the
server's whole machine crashed, the "cleanup" won't happen, thus all
clients must be prepared to deal with "bad" entries anyway.  How about
unexporting as a "favor" to clients (to minimize the latency
associated with discovering the bad entries) then?  Now we're into
more subjective territory.  Arguably if clients did a reasonable job
of long-term caching server "goodness" information (i.e., remembered
not to try to access servers observed to be bad within some interval
of time), then server's unexporting wouldn't be such a favor (and
would have that nasty replication overhead).

Of course one could wish that replication were cheaper.
