In article <CDIL0p.C8E@atria.com>, Nathaniel Mishkin <mishkin@atria.com> wrote:
++ : Unfortunately, WaitForMultipleObjects isn't quite as useful as
++ : select() or poll() since it only reports 1 object of the set of n
++ : objects when it returns 
++ 
++ As long as WaitForMultipleObjects() is reasonably fast when one of the
++ objects is signalled, I guess I wouldn't worry about this difference
++ from select().

	Out of curiosity, under circumstances where many potential
descriptors/handles may become "signaled" during a "waitfor..." isn't
the WIN 32 approach more likely to end up with more starvation (unless
extra steps are taken to avoid it)?  For example, consider the
following canonical use of select() for a network server with many
potential clients, one per descriptor (the poll() approach isn't much
different):

	loop
		set relevant fd's in descriptor mask
		select ()
		foreach enabled descriptor loop
			handle descriptor
		end loop
	end loop

In this approach, when select() returns, the application can handle
all the enabled descriptors in basically any order without (1)
additional intervention with the kernel to determine whether the
descriptor was enabled (hence, probably more efficient) and (2)
without giving preference to any particular descriptor.

Now consider the typical WaitForMultipleObjects approach for a similar
scenario (please tell me if this is an inaccurate portrayal):

	loop
		set up vector of handles
		WaitForMultipleObjects()
		perform operation on the *one* signaled handle
	end loop

Now it strikes me that there are several limitations with this
approach: (1) if there are k handles/descriptors with I/O enabled it's
going to require k calls into the NT kernel to *detect* this (rather
than the 1 required for select()) and (2) if there is always I/O
available on an earlier descriptor (i.e., one that appears towards the
"beginning" of the handle-vector) then won't this handle get served a
great deal more than the other ones?  I can think of ways to get
around this, but it seems like a lot more work!

	Am I missing something wrt the WIN32 NT "multiple wait"
paradigm?

	Doug
