   additional intervention with the kernel to determine whether the
   descriptor was enabled (hence, probably more efficient) and (2)
   without giving preference to any particular descriptor.

There is also a case (3): when the application needs to give preference to
a particular FD.  I recently wrote a real-time application which does a
select() on an arbitrarily large set of descriptors.  One particular FD
requires significantly higher service throughput than the N-1 others, else
a hard failure will occur.  Because select() returns the complete set of
FDs with pending activity, it's within the application's ability to control
service policy.  

And absolutely yes, the Un*x standards folks should merge the file
descriptor and System V IPC identifiers into a unified, select()able space.
It seems such an obvious, upwardly-compatible enhancement.

