In article <THUAN.93Sep17151204@hpltp.hpl.hp.com>,
Thuan Q. Pham <thuan@hpltp.hpl.hp.com> wrote:
>
>In article <27d4tuINNdcq@darkstar.UCSC.EDU> scooper@cse.ucsc.edu (Scott Cooper) writes:

>If you mean kill itself gracefully when you hit ^C, then you have to handle
>the UNIX signals.  To handle this (like control-C), we must start a thread
>to handle the signal.  Couldn't be done otherwise. The thread create a
>signal mask, and actually block with a   call to sigwait().  When a signal
>is present, it unblocks, stops the server from accepting new calls, and
>cause it to exit when the last existing rpc call is handled. (really
>graceful exit).  Then, it breaks the server-listen loop and start the clean
>up routine.  (Just register a signal handling routine by calling signal()
>isn't good enough).

Good explanation, but to clarify:

Call that "quitServer" routine that Thuan described after your call to
rpc_server_listen. In your signal handler, call
rpc_mgmt_stop_server_listening with a binding handle parameter of
NULL. This will make the server stop accepting RPCs and make
rpc_server_listen return.

>>2) Authorized clients should be able to indicate that the server should
>>shut down
>
>Server should support a function that enable a client to call it and tell
>it to kill itself.

No need to bother if you don't feel like it. If you use
rpc_mgmt_stop_server_listening in your signal handler like I described
above, then any client can call it too once it has a binding to your
server. However, your server must call rpc_mgmt_set_authorization_fn
to register a callback that DCE will call whenever the client calls a
management routine. By default, stop_server_listening calls from
remote clients are rejected. Your callback must decide if the client
is authorized, then return "TRUE" if it wants the shutdown request to
be accepted.

				greg


