Very nice analogy.  On that note, it would be nice to for DCE to have a
very light polling function so that we can know whether a binding is bogus
or not.  It's like we can make a phone call to find out you're not home,
instead of driving/walking over and find out first hand.

Right now, the penalty to pay is waiting a few seconds for the time out to
find out that we can't connect to a server, and that's too much.  The client
application seems very lethargic in its behavior.

The point about replication overhead and server stability is well taken; and
even though it's not the popular doctrine, I like Rich's school of thought that:

   > ... namespace should accurately
   > reflect what is going on.  Inefficiencies should be fixed -- and over
   > time will be, if only by getting better hardware :-).
   > 

Until there is a cheap way to find out bad bindings, it'll be hard for some
client application to pay the penalty for bad bindings, especially when the
server can help out by doing clean exit.  If a server crashes, yeah, we can't do
much about that, but shouldn't it help out by cleaning up if possible?

