Douglas C. Schmidt (schmidt@liege.ics.uci.edu) wrote:
: 	Right, we are aware of that.  The next question, then, is
: whether it is permissible to "select()" on arbitrary handles other
: than sockets().  If so, then NT exhibits more orthogonal "selection"
: capabilities than UNIX; if not, then NT has the same problem as UNIX
: i.e., multiple mechanisms each of which only cover a portion of the
: desired solution space.

Not exactly.  I.e., the general selection primitive needn't be
select() itself.  I can imagine some reason that you can't call select
on, say, a console or a named pipe but _could_ call
WaitForMultipleObjects() on either of those or a socket.  For example,
in the course of its implementation, select() might do some
socket-specific operation for some reason related to how the Winsock
API is defined to work.  This could make it fail on non-sockets.
Nevertheless, the WaitForMultipleObjects could work on sockets.
