In <CDrFDG.Cs@jabberwock.NeoSoft.com> daniels@jabberwock.NeoSoft.com (Brad Daniels) writes:
>typedef enum {
>    INT_TYPE, FLOAT_TYPE, DOUBLE_TYPE
>} valType;
>
>typedef struct {
>    union switch (valType t) {
>	case INT_TYPE:		int i;
>	case FLOAT_TYPE:	float f;
>	case DOUBLE_TYPE:	double d;
>    } valUnion;
>} value;
>
>typedef struct {
>    int count;
>    [size_is(count)] value vals[];
>} valList;
>
>[idempotent] void getValues([out] valList *vals);

You were *very* close. :-)  You want to do this:
	void getValues([in, out] valList *vals);

Your client code should look like this:
	valList x;
	x.count = 0;
	getValues(&x);
	for (i = 0; i < x.count)
	    munge(x.vals[i]);
	rpc_ss_client_free(x.vals);

Your server could should look like this:
	getValues(valList *xp)
	{
	    int i;
	    xp->count = get_count();
	    xp->vals = rpc_ss_allocate(xp->count * sizeof xp->vals[0]);
	    for (i = 0; i < xp->count; i++)
		get_nth_value(i, &xp->vals[i]);
	}

The key is that you cannot pass in a top-level conformant array.  You have
to wrap it in a struct, as you did.  The DCE Application Development Guide
says that a structure containing a conformant array must be passed by
reference, must have the [in] attribute, and that the containing structure
can only have one conformant array which must be the last element in the
array.  See "Rules for Using Arrays" (section 17.14.5.3) in Volume 1 of
the guide.

:In either case, how are we actually supposed to handle memory allocation?
:I think we're supposed to just allocate the memory at the client end,
:and pass it in to be filled up by the stub.

You can do this but you'll also have to fill in dummy values or add a
[length_is] attribute and variable so that the memory is allocated, but
the client ships 0 elements and the server ships back the right number of
elements.  Or, you can do as I did above and use the rpc_ss_xxx functions.

A basic rule is that if the server is going to allocate memory that will
be returned to a client, it must use rpc_ss_allocate (rather then malloc).
If a client wants to free memory that was allocated by a stub it should
call rpc_ss_client_free (rather then free).  The client should free
everything that the server allocated.

Hope this helps,
	/r$


