: In article <THUAN.93Sep21135042@hpltp.hpl.hp.com> thuan@hpltp.hpl.hp.com (Thuan Q. Pham) writes:
: > Right now, the penalty to pay is waiting a few seconds for the time out to
: > find out that we can't connect to a server, and that's too much.  The client
: > application seems very lethargic in its behavior.
: True, if you use datagram (UDP) based RPCs. If you use connection-oriented
: (TCP) RPCs, you won't have to wait for the RPC runtime to time out.

True except in case the problem is a network outage or the server host
being down and not just that the particular server process on the
server host is not running.  Arguably the latter case (server process
down) is less common in a well-administered environment running
non-buggy servers.  In the former two cases (network outage or server
host down), you'll have to pay a non-trivial timeout to be fairly
confident that you can't get to the server.  (In particular, there's
no reason why this timeout period wouldn't be the same for RPC/TCP and
RPC/UDP since they're both trying to effect the same policy.)
